<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadiya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amedi, Amadiyah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadiya, or Amedi, is an ancient town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> Region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, built on a flat-topped rock plateau rising sheer above a mountain valley near the Turkish border. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the capital of the Kurdish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Bahdinan, known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholars. Its old western gateway, the Bab Zebar, and a tall medieval minaret survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadiya.jpg" alt="Amadiya" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Army photo by SGT Daniel Nelson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:173606_The_picturesque_village_of_Amedye,_Iraq_in_2009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
